WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.530
History to me is not necessarily
about remembering the dates.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:10.080
It's more about experiencing that
history while I'm learning the language.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:14.610
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here
again, uh, talking about languages

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:18.300
of course, and today I'm going to
talk about languages and history.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.170
Uh, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:23.700
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:28.785
If you follow me on Spotify or
at least any other, um, podcast

00:00:28.785 --> 00:00:30.675
service, please leave a review.

00:00:31.695 --> 00:00:32.415
Why history?

00:00:32.415 --> 00:00:34.125
Why do I want to talk about history?

00:00:34.125 --> 00:00:36.795
Because history fascinates me.

00:00:37.455 --> 00:00:41.715
And I think, you know, I realize that
people watch my videos either because

00:00:41.715 --> 00:00:46.215
they're working on improving their English
or because they find them, you know,

00:00:46.215 --> 00:00:51.945
my videos, in some way motivating and
encouraging and therefore encourages them

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:58.440
to continue with learning languages, which
in turn motivates me to make more videos.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.260
And so I was thinking the
other day, you know, what

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.900
motivates me to learn languages?

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:08.760
So it started with a professor, as
I've said before, that I had at McGill

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.720
university, because we had French
at school, I wasn't very interested.

00:01:12.075 --> 00:01:15.855
Uh, there were, you know, in a city
of 3 million people in those days in

00:01:15.855 --> 00:01:19.845
Montreal, and there were 2 million French
speakers and 1 million English speakers.

00:01:19.845 --> 00:01:24.105
I could have gone out and found people to
talk to if I wanted to, uh, I wasn't very

00:01:24.105 --> 00:01:30.795
motivated until this professer, we had
this course, French civilization, and we

00:01:30.795 --> 00:01:37.035
had this tremendous book and, you know,
between, uh, you know, Moliere and...

00:01:37.065 --> 00:01:42.795
Voltaire and, and reading all these,
uh, bits and pieces of what, some of

00:01:42.795 --> 00:01:49.395
the thinkers among thing and others, or
even seeing, uh, you know, the famous

00:01:49.395 --> 00:01:55.155
painters like David and Watteau and all
of this it for whatever reason it turned

00:01:55.155 --> 00:01:56.635
my crank and maybe it was the professor.

00:01:56.965 --> 00:01:59.445
But it was largely a matter of history.

00:01:59.565 --> 00:02:05.414
I suddenly became conscious of this,
this French civilization, which

00:02:05.445 --> 00:02:10.185
like all civilizations went back
in history, but I became interested

00:02:10.305 --> 00:02:12.585
specifically in French civilization.

00:02:12.704 --> 00:02:18.315
Not that it is any more fascinating
or more valuable than any other

00:02:18.375 --> 00:02:22.065
civilization, but it has its charm.

00:02:22.095 --> 00:02:23.355
Its works of art.

00:02:23.355 --> 00:02:28.334
Its, its architecture, its
literature, its history.

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:32.685
And I can still remember when I first
arrived in Paris, when I had hitchhiked

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:38.654
across the ocean on a, on a small German
tramp steamer and hitchhiked into Paris.

00:02:38.654 --> 00:02:43.274
And I saw the Arc de Triomphe and I
said, I was just beside myself because

00:02:43.274 --> 00:02:47.774
this is this history that I had become
so, uh, you know, interested in.

00:02:48.435 --> 00:02:50.415
Um, and so that was...

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.670
and then I went to France and I
studied for three years and then

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:56.510
the next language was Chinses.

00:02:56.810 --> 00:03:00.704
And I can remember when I got
into Chinese, what fascinated

00:03:00.704 --> 00:03:02.715
me again was history.

00:03:03.105 --> 00:03:07.424
Uh, I remember, of course, when we
first start out, we have to read things

00:03:07.424 --> 00:03:11.204
that are relatively uninteresting, and
I've mentioned that we have to deal

00:03:11.204 --> 00:03:13.915
with relatively uninteresting content

00:03:14.285 --> 00:03:17.995
uh, with a lot of repetition, but
once we're able to start climbing

00:03:17.995 --> 00:03:21.174
our way out of that, and we go
after things of interest, to me

00:03:21.174 --> 00:03:22.975
it's history that I go after.

00:03:23.364 --> 00:03:26.545
And so with Chinese, it
struck me as so romantic.

00:03:26.545 --> 00:03:32.880
The idea that this ancient civilization
of China had all of a sudden come into

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.250
contact with the West, with different
ways of thinking, with science,

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:42.120
with different political systems
and how did they cope with that?

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:48.000
And so for me, some of the literature
and the history of the sort of first

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.900
half of the 20th century in China
was, was absolutely fascinating.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:56.850
And I devoured material on that
subject, unfortunately, in Chinese

00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:02.540
to read about ancient history very
often is more in sort of literary

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:08.210
Chinese and there is just a limitless,
limitless number of names to remember.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:14.480
And I found that while, you know, it was
fascinating to conjure up the Tang dynasty

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:20.630
and how they lived and so forth or other
dynasties, the Han and the Shang, but

00:04:20.659 --> 00:04:24.914
the portion of history that fascinated
me wasthe early part of the 20th century.

00:04:24.914 --> 00:04:25.695
So that was Chinese.

00:04:26.085 --> 00:04:30.344
When I came to Japan, I've mentioned
before I listened over and over to the

00:04:30.344 --> 00:04:36.405
history of the Showa Era, which was
produced by NHK, which had the real voices

00:04:36.405 --> 00:04:40.935
of the real people in radio programs and
elsewhere or radio announcers describing

00:04:40.935 --> 00:04:45.705
events during the Showa Era, which
began...and again, I forget, you know, I

00:04:45.705 --> 00:04:48.960
love history, but I forget it as fast as
I learn it but it was some time in the

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:52.320
twenties up until the second world war.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.770
And then after the second world
war, um, yeah, the Showa Era...

00:04:58.110 --> 00:04:58.620
I can't remember.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:04:59.220
It doesn't matter.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:03.840
History to me is not necessarily
about remembering the dates.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:09.429
It's more about experiencing that
history while I'm learning the language.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:13.510
So through the language, I'm getting
a flavor of the country, which then

00:05:13.599 --> 00:05:16.179
influences my perspective on the country.

00:05:16.659 --> 00:05:18.969
So that was true of, uh, of Japanese.

00:05:20.229 --> 00:05:25.539
And I can recollect, recollect
listening to history, history of Poland.

00:05:25.539 --> 00:05:31.000
As soon as I got through the mini
stories I found on, uh, publio.pl I

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:36.060
think it is, um, a book where I could
get both the audio book and the ebook.

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:38.415
And so I could read it on
LingQ and I could listen to it.

00:05:38.475 --> 00:05:43.845
History of Poland, history of Ukraine,
history of Russia, uh, even certain

00:05:43.845 --> 00:05:49.034
novels, like every Italian person I've
ever met say tells me that they find I

00:05:49.475 --> 00:05:53.655
promessi sposi by Mazzoni very boring
cause they have to read it in school.

00:05:53.835 --> 00:05:58.725
But for me it conjures up
17th century, I believe it is,

00:05:59.235 --> 00:06:01.455
uh, you know, Northern Italy.

00:06:01.485 --> 00:06:03.345
And so again, it's
history and to me, it's...

00:06:04.365 --> 00:06:08.485
I, I remember jogging, uh,
listening to it, I promessi sposi,

00:06:08.715 --> 00:06:10.155
and I listened to a lot of it.

00:06:10.305 --> 00:06:15.525
Wonderful rendition by, uh , il
narratore which is a great site for

00:06:15.525 --> 00:06:17.345
people who are learning Italian.

00:06:18.765 --> 00:06:20.594
I mean every  language,
when I was looking...

00:06:20.805 --> 00:06:22.605
and Greek the same way.

00:06:22.605 --> 00:06:26.325
I found a podcast where they
talked about how the, the, uh,

00:06:26.385 --> 00:06:29.865
Parthenon was built stone by stone.

00:06:30.135 --> 00:06:32.594
And that was very difficult for me
because I didn't have the words,

00:06:32.625 --> 00:06:33.854
but at least it was interesting.

00:06:34.575 --> 00:06:38.205
And so I, and, and even now, you
know, it's, it's so fascinating.

00:06:38.565 --> 00:06:42.555
Like, I, I stopped listening to,
or at least learning Korean because

00:06:42.585 --> 00:06:48.965
there was a lack of content at my
level, but, sort of interesting...

00:06:48.965 --> 00:06:50.820
there was interesting
content it was too difficult.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:54.105
Htere was content that I could
understand that was uninteresting.

00:06:54.915 --> 00:06:58.965
Uh, but lo and behold, my wife has
started watching these historical

00:06:58.965 --> 00:07:01.485
dramas, Korean historical dramas.

00:07:02.145 --> 00:07:04.455
And so that just gets me keen again.

00:07:04.725 --> 00:07:09.735
So there's this one called the, The
Crowned Clown, which is like most

00:07:09.735 --> 00:07:15.285
of these historical dramas, not very
believable, but still fascinating.

00:07:15.285 --> 00:07:19.515
And of course it presents the costumes
and, and in that it's, it's, it's

00:07:19.515 --> 00:07:25.945
that period where the Manchus who are
described there as being the late Jin,

00:07:25.995 --> 00:07:31.664
because the Jin dynasty invaded the
Song, China, a Chinese dynasty back

00:07:31.664 --> 00:07:34.364
in the, I guess the 12th century.

00:07:34.965 --> 00:07:37.995
And this is the same Manchu people.

00:07:38.625 --> 00:07:42.554
Uh, and they're once again, go
invade China and they invade Korea

00:07:43.094 --> 00:07:46.905
and they then become, they start
calling themselves the Manchu and

00:07:46.905 --> 00:07:49.125
they call themselves the Qing Dynasty.

00:07:49.125 --> 00:07:53.190
But the interesting thing there, again,
So I look up on Wikipedia and you see

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:59.100
pictures of the sort of, uh, Ningjiasu
or Ningjiasu however he's pronounced, who

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:05.370
is the leader of the Jin, later Jin and
eventually, uh, which are known in English

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:12.180
as Jurchen or, uh, anyway, so he dresses
like it's all Chinese, culturally Chinese,

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:18.690
and yet the sort of scrolls behind him
are written in this vertical script.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:27.450
Then when I look it up, it's the Mongol
Manchu script, which is an adaptation

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:33.240
of the  Uyghur script, which is in turn
an adaptation of the Sogdian script.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:39.150
The Sogdians are, are an Iranian speaking
people who controlled the silk road.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:46.540
Uh, and that in turn has its origins in
the Middle East, uh, you know, Aramaic

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:50.740
or I don't know, Syriac or Phoenicia,
and there's that it's all connected.

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:53.410
And once you begin, once you
start to realize, after a while

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:55.990
is all history is connected.

00:08:56.530 --> 00:09:01.270
So we tend to, you know, the major
countries in the world, they did all

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:02.620
the history plus our own country.

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:06.310
But of course, you know, the
Manchus had their history, the

00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:07.540
Mongol's have their history.

00:09:07.990 --> 00:09:13.930
Uh, they connect through Central Asia to
Central Asia, which a thousand years ago

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:16.660
was a major center of, of world cultures.

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:22.470
Uh, connecting the Arab world with
the Indian world, with the Persian

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:27.420
world, with the Chinese world,
uh, Persians had a major influence

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:30.540
as did Arabs in the Tang dynasty.

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:36.600
There were many Persian and Arab
traders and, and literary people and

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:39.580
artists in, uh, I guess it was...

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:40.290
I can't remember.

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:42.450
So all of this stuff is connected.

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:48.540
So when I learn Persian now, of course
I, um, I'm just loving because a

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:52.410
lot of the historical stuff is too
difficult, but again, uh, Sahra has

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:59.100
created these 26 episodes of Iranian
history in easier language for me.

00:09:59.189 --> 00:10:00.689
And so I listen to that and read that.

00:10:00.689 --> 00:10:04.199
And, and that course, you know,
stimulates me to go off and read

00:10:04.199 --> 00:10:07.230
about the history of Persia elsewhere.

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:10.199
So I just want to pass that along.

00:10:10.199 --> 00:10:12.520
I mean, I could go on, I'm
trying to think of other...

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:17.250
like Ukrainian, again, to understand
Ukraine, you have to read Ukrainian

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:20.790
history and you have to read Ukrainian
history written by Ukrainians.

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:24.810
So if you're going to do that, then
may as well read it in Ukrainian and

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:28.800
may as well listen to the audio book
in Ukrainian, which I have done.

00:10:29.069 --> 00:10:33.449
And so that gives you a perspective
on Ukraine, which is different from

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:39.540
a perspective on Ukrainian history
written by someone from Poland because

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:44.180
they dominated Ukraine for hundreds of
years, or written by someone from Russia.

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:47.280
Russia also dominated portions
of Ukraine for many years.

00:10:47.939 --> 00:10:54.240
And so when you get the history of
the people spoken by the people in

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:57.840
their language and you can access it
and their history and their culture

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:03.630
and their various, uh, cultural
creations throughout their history,

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:06.930
that motivates me to learn a language.

00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:09.900
It's not simply the fact that I
might be able to speak to someone.

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:13.530
Of course, I want to speak to someone,
but the motivation for me in these

00:11:13.530 --> 00:11:22.454
languages is largely history and,
uh, watching that Netflix video the

00:11:22.454 --> 00:11:28.305
other day has gotten me motivated
again to go and brush up my Korean.

00:11:28.364 --> 00:11:29.535
We'll see what happens.

00:11:29.805 --> 00:11:33.435
All these competing, uh, you know,
attractive languages to learn.

00:11:33.824 --> 00:11:35.444
Anyway, I just wanted
to share that with you.

00:11:35.685 --> 00:11:40.905
Uh, thank you for listening and
remember to get a, to, to, to

00:11:40.905 --> 00:11:43.604
achieve your goals and language
learning, you have to be motivated.

00:11:43.604 --> 00:11:45.314
You have to find things that motivate you.

00:11:45.964 --> 00:11:51.180
Uh, and you have to read and listen
on those subjects of interest and I'll

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:53.430
leave a few videos that relate to that.

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:54.480
Thanks for listening.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:55.050
Bye for now.

